defmodule <%= @module %> do
  @moduledoc """
  Native Mob port of Mishka Chelekom's **headless Pill** — a compact label with
  an optional trailing remove button (a token, a tag, a filter you can dismiss).

  ## Pill or Chip?

  They look alike and are easy to confuse, so the port keeps them distinct in the
  way that matters: a **Chip is selected**, a **Pill is removed**. A chip carries
  `checked` and toggles; a pill carries content and offers a ✕. If you find
  yourself giving a pill a checked state, you want
  `<%= @namespace %>.<%= @module_prefix_camel %>Chip`.

  ## The remove button is its own tap target

  `with_remove` renders a ✕ that carries its own handler, so tapping the label
  and tapping the ✕ are different events — the pill itself can stay inert while
  only its ✕ responds, which is the usual behaviour for a token in an input.

  ## Props

  | Prop | Values | Default | Meaning |
  |------|--------|---------|---------|
  | `label` | string | `nil` | The pill's text. Children override it. |
  | `with_remove` | boolean | `false` | Render the trailing ✕. |
  | `on_remove` | event tag (atom) | — | Sent as `{:tap, tag}` when the ✕ is tapped. |
  | `on_tap` | event tag (atom) | — | Makes the pill body itself tappable. |
  | `disabled` | boolean | `false` | Mutes it and wires no handlers, including the ✕. |
  | `background` | color token / ARGB int | `:surface_raised` | Pill fill. |
  | `color` | color token / ARGB int | `:on_surface` | Label colour. |
  | `disabled_color` | color token / ARGB int | `:muted` | Label colour while disabled. |
  | `corner_radius` | radius token / number | `:radius_pill` | Corner rounding. |
  | `padding` | spacing token / number | `:space_sm` | Inside the pill, all four sides. |
  | `padding_top` / `padding_right` / `padding_bottom` / `padding_left` | spacing token / number | unset | One edge, overriding `padding` on that side only. |
  | `width` | number | unset | Exact width. Unset, the pill hugs its label. |
  | `height` | number | unset | Exact height. Unset, the pill hugs its label. |
  | `fill_width` | boolean | `false` | Span the parent instead of hugging. |
  | `align` | `:center`, `:leading`, `:trailing`, … | unset (top-leading) | Where the content sits when `width` / `height` leave it room. |
  | `border_color` | color token / ARGB int | unset | Outline colour. Draws only alongside `border_width`. |
  | `border_width` | number | unset (no outline) | Outline width. |
  | `shadow` | shadow spec string | unset | Passed straight through to the root Box — see "Shadows" below. |
  | `text_size` | text token / number | `:base` | Label size. |
  | `font_weight` | `:regular`, `:medium`, `:semibold`, `:bold`, `:light`, `:thin` | unset (regular) | Label weight — and the ✕'s. |
  | `letter_spacing` | number (sp) | unset | Tracking on the label. |
  | `line_height` | number | unset | Multiplier of `text_size`; ignored without one. |
  | `remove_gap` | number | `8` | Space between the label and the ✕. |
  | `remove_size` | text token / number | whatever `text_size` is | The ✕'s size, when it should differ from the label's. |
  | `leading` | string, node, or list of nodes | unset (nothing) | Content *before* the label — a dot, an icon, a count. See "The leading slot". |
  | `leading_gap` | number | `8` | Space between `leading` and the label. Only reaches the tree when `leading` does. |
  | `leading_size` | text token / number | whatever `text_size` is | A **string** `leading`'s size, when it should differ from the label's. Ignored by a node. |
  | `content_align` | `:top`, `:center`, `:bottom` | `:center` | Cross-axis alignment inside the pill's content row. See "The content row centres". |
  | `content_fill_width` | boolean | `false` | The content row spans the pill instead of hugging. See "A `weight` is read by the row above it". |

  Every prop below `disabled_color` is a later addition and every one of them
  defaults to exactly what the pill drew before it existed: the ones with a
  token default (`corner_radius`, `padding`, `text_size`, `remove_gap`) name the
  value that used to be hardcoded, and the ones marked *unset* are **left off
  the node entirely** rather than passed as `nil` — not the same thing once the
  tree is serialised, as the note beside the private `overrides` explains.

  The last four hold to the same rule by two different routes. `leading_gap`
  names no earlier value — nothing rendered in front of the label before
  `leading` existed — so it borrows `remove_gap`'s 8, and no `Spacer` reaches
  the tree unless `leading` puts one there. `content_align` and
  `content_fill_width` are defaults in the *documentation* only: each names what
  the content `Row` already does unprompted, so neither is written onto the node
  until a caller asks for the other thing.

  Not ported: `remove_label` (an `aria-label` for the ✕ — Mob exposes no
  accessible name on a text node) and the `id` / `*_class` attrs.

  ## Sizing: padding is applied BEFORE width and height

  The bridge builds the node's modifier chain padding-first, so `width` and
  `height` measure the box *inside* the padding: `height: 30, padding: 8` is 46
  tall on screen, not 30. Pin the outer dimension by pairing the two —
  `height: 14, padding: 8` — or drop the padding on the axis you are pinning.

  A number is required; `width`/`height` are not spacing props, so a token like
  `:space_md` reaches the bridge unresolved and is ignored. Negative padding
  throws on the native side, so never compute one.

  ## Text size takes a number, and a number skips the type scale

  `text_size` (and `remove_size`) accept a token — `:sm`, `:base` — or a raw
  number in sp, which is what a design pinned to 11.5 needs. Only the token form
  is multiplied by the theme's `type_scale`, so a numeric size stays put when the
  user scales their type. That is the trade: exactness against accessibility.

  ## Shadows

  `shadow` is not interpreted here — it rides on the root Box for the bridge to
  read, in CSS `box-shadow` order (`"dx dy blur spread #AARRGGBB"`, further
  layers after a pipe). A bridge without shadow support ignores the prop, as it
  ignores any key it does not know, so passing one is never a rendering risk.

  ## It hugs its label

  The root Box passes `fill_width={false}` by default, so a pill is exactly as
  wide as its text plus padding. That is what lets several sit in one Row the way
  they do on the web. It matters because a Box with neither `width` nor
  `fill_width` fills its parent, which made every pill a full-width bar. Pass
  `fill_width={true}` for a pill that really should span its container.

  ## The content row centres, and `content_align` is how you stop it

  Everything inside the pill — the `leading` slot, the label, the ✕ — sits in
  one `Row`, and a Mob `Row` aligns its children **centred on the cross axis**
  unless told otherwise (`MobNode.h`: `"top" | "center" (default) | "bottom"`).
  For a one-line token that is invisible, and right. For a note — a 5pt dot or a
  16pt icon beside a paragraph that wraps to three lines — it is wrong in a way
  no other prop can reach: the icon floats to the middle of the block instead of
  sitting on the first line, and the caller has no way to say so.

  `content_align={:top}` says so, and it reaches **both** rows that can hold
  content: the pill's own content row, and the row that children passed to
  `pill/2` are wrapped in. Whichever way the content got in, one prop aligns it.
  (The iOS renderer additionally maps `:baseline` to the last text baseline;
  treat that value as iOS-only unless you have checked the bridge you ship.)

  Because `:center` is the `Row`'s own default, an unset `content_align` writes
  no `align` at all and the tree stays the one the pill always built.

  ## A `weight` is read by the row above it, not by the pill

  `weight` is a *layout* prop that a `Row` reads off its own direct children, so
  which row a child hangs from decides what its weight means. Inside a pill a
  child passed to `pill/2` hangs from the wrapper `Row` in `label/3` — and that
  row, like the content row above it, **hugs**. A paragraph marked
  `weight={1.0}` is therefore sharing out the leftover of a row that has no
  leftover, which reads as "the weight did nothing" even on a `fill_width={true}`
  pill: the root `Box` fills, the rows inside it do not.

  `content_align` does not touch this. Alignment is the **cross** axis and weight
  is the **main** one — different questions — and the answer to the second is
  `content_fill_width={true}`. It puts `fill_width={true}` on the content row, so
  there is a pill's width to share, and `weight={1}` on the children wrapper, so
  the wrapper claims that width as *leftover* rather than as a maximum.

  Leftover is the part that matters when `with_remove` is also set. Compose
  measures a row's un-weighted children first, handing each one the space the
  previous ones did not take: a wrapper that asked for the full width would be
  measured before the ✕ and leave it nothing, and a `Text` that carries
  `TextOverflow.Ellipsis` and has no room renders as nothing at all. A weighted
  wrapper is measured *after* the ✕ and takes what is left of the row.

  Two things follow. It only makes sense on a pill that is itself full width: a
  `Row` that fills inside a `Box` that hugs drags the Box's incoming maximum out
  with it, which is the full-width-bar bug `fill_width={false}` exists to
  prevent — so pass `fill_width={true}` alongside it. And on iOS the `weight`
  half is inert, because `weight` is read nowhere in the iOS renderer
  (`IOS_TODO.md` item 13): the wrapper hugs there exactly as it does today, which
  is the current behaviour rather than a regression.

  ## The leading slot

  `with_remove` gives the pill a trailing ✕; `leading` is the other side of the
  same row. Before it, a caller who wanted a coloured dot in front of the text
  had to pass the whole thing as **children** — and children replace the label
  outright, taking `label`, `text_size`, `font_weight`, `letter_spacing`,
  `line_height` and the `max_lines: 1` guard with them. Five points of dot cost
  every one of the label's typographic props, which the caller then had to
  restate on a `Text` of their own.

  `leading` is a slot in the chip's sense, so it takes whatever the caller has:
  a node (the dot, an icon), a list of nodes, or a string — and a string is
  drawn in the pill's own ink and `text_size`, following `font_weight` the way
  the ✕ does, so a leading glyph matches its label without being told to. It
  does not follow `letter_spacing`, for the ✕'s reason: tracking is applied
  after the last character too, which on a lone glyph is just a gap.

  `leading_gap` (default `8` — `remove_gap`'s value, on the other side) sits
  between the slot and the label. Unset, `leading` puts **nothing** in the tree
  — not an empty `Row`, nothing — so a pill that does not use it builds the
  two-child content row it always did.
  """

  import Mob.Sigil

  alias <%= @namespace %>.Event

  # Root-Box props that must be ABSENT when the caller says nothing, rather than
  # present-and-nil. See `overrides/2` for why the difference is visible.
  @box_overrides ~w(
    width height align shadow border_color border_width
    padding_top padding_right padding_bottom padding_left
  )a

  # Typography, absent for the same reason. `letter_spacing` and `line_height`
  # have no safe neutral value at all — Compose resolves an unset one from the
  # ambient text style, and Material's body style is not 0 / 1.0, so writing
  # those "neutral" numbers would visibly retrack and re-lead every existing
  # pill. `font_weight` does have one (`:regular`), and is still left off, so
  # that a pill nobody restyled builds the map it always built, key for key.
  @text_overrides ~w(font_weight letter_spacing line_height)a

  # A lone glyph — the ✕, or a string `leading` — follows the label's weight but
  # not its tracking: letter-spacing is applied after every character including
  # the last, which on a lone glyph is just a gap between it and its neighbour.
  @glyph_overrides ~w(font_weight)a

  @doc """
  Composite expander (`<<%= @module_prefix_camel %>Pill>`). Children replace the `label` prop.
  """
  @spec expand(map(), [map()], map()) :: map()
  def expand(props, children, _ctx), do: <%= @component_prefix %>pill(props, children)

  @doc """
  The pill node. `content` overrides the `label` prop when given.

      <%= @component_prefix %>pill(label: "elixir", with_remove: true, on_remove: {:drop, :elixir})

  Sized to a design rather than to its text — this one is 30pt tall, because
  padding is applied before height and 9 + 12 + 9 is what the bridge measures:

      <%= @component_prefix %>pill(label: "PRO", height: 12, padding_top: 9, padding_bottom: 9,
           padding_left: 12, padding_right: 12, text_size: 11.5,
           font_weight: :semibold, align: :center)

  A dot in front of the label, without giving up the label's typography:

      <%= @component_prefix %>pill(label: "Live", leading: dot_node, leading_gap: 6)

  A note: an icon on the first line of a paragraph that wraps to the pill's
  width. `content_align` puts the icon on the top; `content_fill_width` is what
  gives the paragraph's own `weight` a row wide enough to share.

      <%= @component_prefix %>pill(%{fill_width: true, content_align: :top, content_fill_width: true},
           [icon_node, paragraph_node])
  """
  @spec <%= @component_prefix %>pill(map() | keyword(), [map()]) :: map()
  def <%= @component_prefix %>pill(props \\ %{}, content \\ []) do
    props = Map.new(props)
    disabled? = truthy?(Map.get(props, :disabled, false))

    node = ~MOB"""
    <Box
      background={Map.get(props, :background, :surface_raised)}
      corner_radius={Map.get(props, :corner_radius, :radius_pill)}
      padding={Map.get(props, :padding, :space_sm)}
      fill_width={truthy?(Map.get(props, :fill_width, false))}
    >
      {content_row(props, content, disabled?)}
    </Box>
    """

    node
    |> merge_props(overrides(props, @box_overrides))
    |> wire(tap(props, disabled?, :on_tap))
  end

  # The one row every part of the pill sits in. `leading/2` is `nil` when the
  # slot is empty and the sigil drops a nil child, so an untouched pill's row
  # still holds exactly the label and the remove row, in that order.
  defp content_row(props, content, disabled?) do
    ~MOB"""
    <Row>
      {leading(props, disabled?)}
      {label(props, content, disabled?)}
      {remove(props, disabled?)}
    </Row>
    """
    |> merge_props(row_props(props))
  end

  # `align` and `fill_width` on a content row, both ABSENT unless asked for —
  # `:center` and hugging are what a Mob `Row` already does, so writing either
  # value would be a no-op on the screen and a difference in the tree.
  defp row_props(props) do
    %{}
    |> put_align(Map.get(props, :content_align))
    |> put_fill(truthy?(Map.get(props, :content_fill_width, false)))
  end

  defp put_align(row_props, nil), do: row_props
  defp put_align(row_props, align), do: Map.put(row_props, :align, align)

  defp put_fill(row_props, false), do: row_props
  defp put_fill(row_props, true), do: Map.put(row_props, :fill_width, true)

  # max_lines: 1 — a pill is a single-line token by nature, and the failure mode
  # without it is ugly rather than merely tight: a Compose Text squeezed narrower
  # than its content wraps CHARACTER BY CHARACTER, so a pill that does not quite
  # fit its row renders as a vertical stack of letters. Ellipsised is wrong by a
  # little; stacked letters look broken. It stays fixed for that reason — the
  # dimension props above change the pill's shape, never its line count.
  defp label(props, [], disabled?) do
    ~MOB"""
    <Text
      text={Map.get(props, :label)}
      text_size={text_size(props)}
      text_color={color(props, disabled?)}
      max_lines={1}
    />
    """
    |> merge_props(overrides(props, @text_overrides))
  end

  # Children replace the label, and they get a Row of their own so that several
  # of them lay out horizontally. That row — not the pill, and not the content
  # row above it — is what reads a `weight` off a child, so it is the row
  # `content_fill_width` has to reach. It reaches it as `weight={1}` rather than
  # as `fill_width`, so a ✕ beside it is measured first and keeps its width;
  # see "A `weight` is read by the row above it".
  defp label(props, content, _disabled?) do
    ~MOB"""
    <Row>
      {content}
    </Row>
    """
    |> merge_props(wrapper_props(props))
  end

  defp wrapper_props(props) do
    %{}
    |> put_align(Map.get(props, :content_align))
    |> put_weight(truthy?(Map.get(props, :content_fill_width, false)))
  end

  defp put_weight(row_props, false), do: row_props
  defp put_weight(row_props, true), do: Map.put(row_props, :weight, 1)

  # The mirror of `remove/2`: slot then gap, rather than gap then glyph. An
  # empty slot is `nil` — not an empty `Row` — because the tree a pill without
  # a `leading` builds has to stay the tree it built before the prop existed,
  # and `remove/2`'s empty `<Row />` is already in that tree and cannot leave.
  defp leading(props, disabled?) do
    case slot(props, disabled?) do
      nil ->
        nil

      slot_node ->
        ~MOB"""
        <Row>
          {slot_node}
          <Spacer size={Map.get(props, :leading_gap, 8)} />
        </Row>
        """
    end
  end

  # A slot takes what the caller has: a node, a list of nodes, or a string. The
  # list is spliced into the row `leading/2` already builds rather than nested
  # in one of its own — the gap is a sibling of the slot either way.
  defp slot(props, disabled?) do
    case Map.get(props, :leading) do
      nil -> nil
      [] -> nil
      nodes when is_list(nodes) -> nodes
      %{type: _type} = node -> node
      text -> slot_text(props, text, disabled?)
    end
  end

  # A string slot is drawn exactly as the ✕ is — the pill's ink, the label's
  # size unless `leading_size` says otherwise, the label's weight but not its
  # tracking — so that a leading glyph matches the label it precedes without
  # the caller restating a single typographic prop.
  defp slot_text(props, text, disabled?) do
    ~MOB"""
    <Text
      text={to_string(text)}
      text_size={Map.get(props, :leading_size, text_size(props))}
      text_color={color(props, disabled?)}
    />
    """
    |> merge_props(overrides(props, @glyph_overrides))
  end

  # The ✕ is a separate tap target from the pill body, so a token can be
  # removable without the whole pill being tappable.
  #
  # It follows the label's size and weight unless `remove_size` says otherwise:
  # a semibold 11.5sp pill with a regular 16sp ✕ reads as a mistake, and the
  # fallback chain keeps the unstyled pill at the `:base` it always drew.
  defp remove(props, disabled?) do
    if truthy?(Map.get(props, :with_remove, false)) do
      node = ~MOB"""
      <Row>
        <Spacer size={Map.get(props, :remove_gap, 8)} />
        {glyph(props, disabled?)}
      </Row>
      """

      wire(node, tap(props, disabled?, :on_remove))
    else
      ~MOB(<Row />)
    end
  end

  defp glyph(props, disabled?) do
    ~MOB"""
    <Text
      text="✕"
      text_size={Map.get(props, :remove_size, text_size(props))}
      text_color={color(props, disabled?)}
    />
    """
    |> merge_props(overrides(props, @glyph_overrides))
  end

  defp text_size(props), do: Map.get(props, :text_size, :base)

  defp color(props, true), do: Map.get(props, :disabled_color, :muted)
  defp color(props, _), do: Map.get(props, :color, :on_surface)

  defp tap(_props, true, _key), do: nil
  defp tap(props, _disabled, key), do: Event.handler(Map.get(props, key))

  defp wire(node, nil), do: node
  defp wire(node, handler), do: %{node | props: Map.put(node.props, :on_tap, handler)}

  # A prop the caller never set must be ABSENT from the node, not present as
  # `nil`. The tree is serialised with `:json.encode/1`, which renders the atom
  # `nil` as the STRING "nil" — so a defaulted `height: nil` would reach the
  # bridge as `"height":"nil"`, and a bridge that coerces with -doubleValue
  # reads that as 0.0 and collapses the pill. Taking only the keys the caller
  # supplied is also what makes an untouched pill's props map identical, key for
  # key, to the one it built before any of these props existed.
  defp overrides(props, keys) do
    props
    |> Map.take(keys)
    |> Map.reject(fn {_key, value} -> is_nil(value) end)
  end

  defp merge_props(node, extra) when map_size(extra) == 0, do: node
  defp merge_props(node, extra), do: %{node | props: Map.merge(node.props, extra)}

  defp truthy?(nil), do: false
  defp truthy?(false), do: false
  defp truthy?(_), do: true
end
